i grew up in manhattan i grew up on the  west side  and around the dinner table i heard  stories and one of the stories was  that my parents were leaving berlin  they had a six-week visa to come to new  york to work  my father asked his mother for uh  jewelry  and he bought a box of chocolate melted  the chocolate down  and covered all of the pieces of jewelry  in chocolate put it in the box put the  box under his arm  so that when the nazis said are you  taking anything of value  out of germany he said no check my bags  check us nothing he then got to new york  city  and he pawned the jewelry  eventually started a lumber business and  was able to buy the jewelry back and  i have the pocket watch  from my great-grandfather  that was smuggled out of germany  uh encased in chocolate still works  um just an amazing  thing to have to pass down from  generation  to generation okay so same apartment  manhattan on every windowsill  was a spider plant the legend was  that the mother of these spider plants  came out in a coffin at the feet  of tanta and akulman  now i never really had real aunts and  uncles because they were all taken  i never had grandparents because they  were all  taken to the the camps but we did have  these plants now when i came in 1973  to california and i auditioned two weeks  in to my stay here  i auditioned for happy days and when i  got  the part when i when we were going to do  the pilot  and then all of a sudden we were picked  up came out here  to do the first season lived in l.a  and i brought with me a cutting from the  windowsill  of one of those spider plants and  this is the result so it's been growing  and as you can see  um you know it has all these babies  and then you plant these babies and  um i did so this plant  the mother of this plant came out of  germany  in a coffin at the feet  of anna cullman and uh then  i took babies and i put them in pots  so that each of my children have a baby  to plant in their garden to uh  continue the tradition  [Music]
 

